T-NEWB-SUMMARY [ed0c08e297b4] Test harnesses boot the server on a data folder with no persistence sentinel, so the volume-wiped alarm fires in tests (127 lines in one generator log; a first-boot banner adds 18 lines to the contrast suite)
T-NEWB-P1 [67076164741f] BLUF: two independent reviews of this week found the same harness fault from two sides. (1) The builder of RD-1021 saw 127 'Persistence sentinel MISSING' lines in the inventory generator's log, and the review's re-run printed them again in the RD-1011 test boots: the harness writes its machine marker before the server starts, so the product's volume check finds a data folder that already exists with no sentinel and raises the alarm RD-404 added for a wiped volume. (2) The end-to-end suite's start script scripts/qa-surface-up.sh copies the dataset but not the sentinel file (the file names the sentinel 0 times; it names the dataset file 4 times), so a first boot of the local surface shows a 'Persistent storage check failed' banner whose Open Settings link reads 2 point 48 to 1 and adds 9 lines in each mode to the contrast suite's list; the suite must be run on the second boot. Nothing is claimed about the product: the alarm is right for a real wiped volume. The harness noise trains readers to ignore a real data-loss alarm.
T-NEWB-P2 [8032259eb5a3] Where: the counts are the reports' (the 127 lines are the builder's, relayed by the review; the banner and its 18 lines are the review's of the contrast suite); this ticket's author read only the start script (scripts/qa-surface-up.sh at the merge commit of pull request 247, whose first 8 characters are 04a6b89d) and did not run any harness. Where the alarm text is raised in the product was not read. Recommendation (a proposal, not decided): have the harnesses seed the sentinel with the dataset (or mark the boot as a test boot), and have the contrast suite refuse a first-boot surface instead of measuring the banner. Low proposed: test tooling only.
